Philip Potter wrote:
> sjdevnull@yahoo.com wrote:
> It certainly breaks many coding standards which are commonplace today - 
> the biggest being that it uses global variables left, right and centre. 
> Another is that a lot of grid bug no-diagonal movement code is hardcoded 
> into various routines throughout the program.

Using global variables is not bad thing if the entity it represents is 
global. Also big structures and often used strings are in C usually in 
golbal variables.

The PC, the playground and the game state are global entities and thus 
should be represented by global variables.

Looking through decl.h I only noticed that some information is 
transfered between various code parts using global variables and this is 
not necessary: killer information, monster non-melee attack directions 
and multipleshots are passed in global variables.

I think multishot is in global variable because it was late addition. It 
was easier to code as an global variable than change all the calls that 
handle non-melee combat.

